---
export const prerender = true;
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";

// Draft prose grounded in the real facts already in resume.tsx (Sisters of St.
// Joseph responsibilities, program sites led).
---

<Layout title="About" description="Nonprofit background, Sisters of St. Joseph, and where I'm headed next.">
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-6 py-16 sm:py-20">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-foreground sm:text-4xl">About</h1>

    <div class="mt-10 flex flex-col gap-12 text-foreground">
      <section>
        <p class="leading-relaxed">
          I've spent most of my career as the technical department nonprofits don't have
          budget to hire - the person who builds the site, runs the fundraising, and picks
          up whatever else the mission actually needs. That's meant working close to the
          people running these organizations, not just shipping a deliverable and leaving.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Sisters of St. Joseph</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed">
          My title at Sisters of St. Joseph was Website Manager, but that title
          undersold the job. I was the organization's primary technical support for its
          WordPress website, with donation forms powered by Blackbaud Raiser's Edge - giving
          pages that had to work correctly, not just look right. I managed their Constant Contact email
          program and social presence out of the Communications Office. And I designed and
          developed three separate program sites - the Long Island Immigration Clinic, the
          Legacy Fund, and Spirit Alive - each working directly with the program director
          who ran it. I built them myself in WordPress with Blackbaud's drag-and-drop editor,
          often writing HTML and CSS for smaller styling jobs.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed">
          I also designed and built the website for their fourth program, CSJ Learning
          Connection, on a freelance contract. Before designing anything, I did community
          research - talking with the program's students, teachers, and donors about what they
          needed from the site, then testing a prototype with them. The final site was built on
          Webflow as its own standalone platform, with a modern design, animations, a language
          switcher, and a Raisely donation form I implemented.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Cat Connections NYC</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed">
          I founded Cat Connections NYC, an Astoria-based cat rescue, and built its
          digital presence from nothing - a real home online where the organization
          could showcase adoptable and adopted cats, tell its story, and actually
          convert visitors into donors and volunteers, not just describe the mission.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed">
          As founder, I served as technical lead, fundraising director, and social media manager,
          owning the website end to end alongside branding, social media, and
          fundraising strategy. I built the site on Webflow CMS with a structured
          content model so adoptable cats, campaigns, and impact stories could be
          updated without a developer.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed">
          On the fundraising side, I ran campaigns across multiple platforms at once -
          Raisely, Zeffy, GoFundMe, Reddit, Instagram, and Facebook - rather than
          relying on a single channel, including community-driven posts on Reddit, a
          channel most nonprofit sites ignore entirely. That effort raised $18,000 in
          five months, including a Fidelity Corporate Giving match.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Freelance Nonprofit Clients</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed">
          Alongside my full-time work, I consulted, designed (often in Figma), and built
          websites for nonprofits directly, using WordPress or Webflow depending on what
          each organization actually needed. That work included <strong>CSJ Learning
          Connection</strong>, <strong>ParaGenius Foundation</strong>, <strong>Sean
          Hanna Foundation</strong>, <strong>and Uptown &amp; Booggie</strong>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Where I'm headed</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed">
          I hope to bring my skills to a nonprofit dedicated to animal welfare. Animals have
          always been my biggest passion - it's why I built Wildari Planet, a passion project
          about wildlife, and why I founded and ran my own small cat rescue, Cat Connections NYC.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed">
          That hasn't stopped. In my free time, I still foster and volunteer with Habitant Cats
          of New York, a local cat rescue.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
